roll,  ang_vel_bias_pitch, ang_vel_bias_yaw]    ]\nL779: where the accleration components are in m/s and the angular velocity is in rad/s.\nL780: \nL781: Configuration\nL782: \nL783: The `configuration` block (see cite34†Configuration Block ) accepts the following options:\nL784: \nL785:   * `AccelSigma`/`AccelCov`: Covariance/Std for acceleration component. Can be scalar, 3-vector or 3x3-matrix. Set one or the other. Defaults to 0 => no noise.\nL786:   * `AngVelSigma`/`AngVelCov`: Covariance/Std for angular velocity component. Can be scalar, 3-vector or 3x3-matrix. Set one or the other. Defaults to 0 => no noise.\nL787: \nL788:   * `AccelBiasSigma`/`AccelCBiasov`: Covariance/Std for acceleration bias component. Can be scalar, 3-vector or 3x3-matrix. Set one or the other. Defaults to 0 => no noise.\nL789:   * `AngVelBiasSigma`/`AngVelBiasCov`: Covariance/Std for acceleration bias component. Can be scalar, 3-vector or 3x3-matrix. Set one or the other. Defaults to 0 => no noise.\nL790: \nL791:   * `ReturnBias`: Whether the sensor should return the bias along with accel/ang. vel. Defaults to false.\nL792: \nL793: Attributes:\nL794: \nL795: cite69†`data_shape` | The shape of the sensor data\nL796: cite70†`dtype` | The type of data in the sensor\nL797: \nL798: property data_shapecite69† L799: \nL800: \nL801: The shape of the sensor data\nL802: \nL803: Returns:\nL804: \nL805: \nL806: Sensor data shape\nL807: \nL808: Return type:\nL809: `tuple`\nL810: \nL811: property dtypecite70† L812: \nL813: \nL814: The type of data in the sensor\nL815: \nL816: Returns:\nL817: \nL818: \nL819: Type of sensor data\nL820: \nL821: Return type:\nL822: \nL823: \nL824: numpy dtype\nL825: \nL826: class holoocean.sensors.ImagingSonar(client, agent_name, agent_type, name='ImagingSonar', config=None)cite13† L827: \nL828: \nL829: Simulates an imaging sonar. See cite71†Octree Configuration for more on how to configure the octree that is used.\nL830: \nL831: Configuration\nL832: \nL833: The `configuration` block (see cite34†Configuration Block ) accepts any of the options in the following sections.\nL834: \nL835: Basic Configuration\nL836:   * `Azimuth`: Azimuth (side to side) angle visible in degrees, defaults to 120.\nL837: \nL838:   * `Elevation`: Elevation angle (up and down) visible in degrees, defaults to 20.\nL839: \nL840:   * `RangeMin`: Minimum range visible in meters, defaults to 0.1.\nL841: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSensors — HoloOcean 2.3.0 documentation (https://byu-holoocean.github.io/holoocean-docs/v2.3.0/holoocean/sensors.html)\nciteturn12view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn11view0\",\"pattern\":\"RGBCamera\"}); Total lines: 1997\nL22: cite13†`ImagingSonar` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Simulates an imaging sonar.\nL23: cite14†`LocationSensor` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Gets the location of the agent in the world.\nL24: cite15†`MagnetometerSensor` (client, agent_name, ...)  | Gets the global x-axis (or given vector) in the local frame.\nL25: cite16†`OpticalModemSensor` (client, agent_name, ...)  | Handles communication between agents using an optical modem.\nL26: cite17†`OrientationSensor` (client[, agent_name, ...])  | Gets the forward, right, and up vector for the agent.\nL27: cite18†`PoseSensor` (client[, agent_name, agent_type, ...])  | Gets the forward, right, and up vector for the agent.\nL28: cite19†`ProfilingSonar` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Simulates a multibeam profiling sonar.\nL29: cite20†`RGBCamera` (client, agent_name, agent_type[, ...])  | Captures agent's view.\nL30: cite21†`RangeFinderSensor` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Returns distances to nearest collisions in the directions specified by the parameters.\nL31: cite22†`RaycastLidar` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Runs Agent's Lidar\nL32: cite23†`RaycastSemanticLidar` (client, agent_name, ...)  | Runs Agent's Semantic Lidar\nL33: cite24†`RotationSensor` (client[, agent_name, ...])  | Gets the rotation of the agent in the world, with rotation XYZ about the fixed frame, in degrees.\nL34: cite25†`SemanticSegmentationCamera` (client, ...[, ...])  | Captures agent's view.\nL35: cite26†`SensorDefinition` (agent_name, agent_type, ...)  | A class for new sensors and their parameters, to be used for adding new sensors.\nL36: cite27†`SensorFactory` ()  | Given a sensor definition, constructs the appropriate HoloOceanSensor object.\nL37: cite28†`SidescanSonar` (client, agent_name, agent_type)  | Simulates a sidescan sonar.\nL1193:   * `RangeSigma`: Additive noise std from an exponential distribution that will be added to the range measurements, and the intensities will be scaled by the pdf. Needs to be a float. Defaults to 0, or off.\nL1194: \nL1195: Advanced Configuration\nL1196: \nL1197:   * `ShowWarning`: Whether to show on screen warning about sonar computation happening. Defaults to True.\nL1198:   * `ElevationBins`/`ElevationRes`: Number of elevation bins used when shadowing is done, or resolution (length in degrees) of each bin. Set one or the other. By default this is computed based on the octree size and the min/max range. Should only be set if shadowing isn’t working.\nL1199: \nL1200:   * `InitOctreeRange`: Upon startup, all mid-level octrees within this distance of the agent will be created.\nL1201: \nL1202:   * `ViewRegion`: Turns on green lines to see visible region. Defaults to False.\nL1203:   * `ViewOctree`: What octree leaves to show. Less than -1 means none, -1 means all, and anything greater than or equal to 0 shows the corresponding beam index. Defaults to -10.\nL1204: \nL1205:   * `ShadowEpsilon`: What constitutes a break between clusters when shadowing. Defaults to 4*OctreeMin.\nL1206: \nL1207:   * `WaterDensity`: Density of water in kg/m^3. Defaults to 997.\nL1208: \nL1209:   * `WaterSpeedSound`: Speed of sound in water in m/s. Defaults to 1480.\nL1210:   * `UseApprox`: Whether to use the faster approximation of Atan2 or the slower exact implementation. Defaults to True.\nL1211: \nL1212: class holoocean.sensors.RGBCamera(client, agent_name, agent_type, name='RGBCamera', config=None)cite20† L1213: \nL1214: \nL1215: Captures agent’s view.\nL1216: \nL1217: The default capture resolution is 256x256x256x4, corresponding to the RGBA channels. The resolution can be increased, but will significantly impact performance.\nL1218: \nL1219: Configuration\nL1220: The `configuration` block (see cite34†Configuration Block ) accepts the following options:\nL1221: \nL1222:   * `CaptureWidth`: Width of captured image\nL1223: \nL1224:   * `CaptureHeight`: Height of captured image\nL1225: \nL1226: Attributes:\nL1227: \nL1228: cite85†`data_shape` | The shape of the sensor data\nL1229: cite86†`dtype` | The type of data in the sensor\nL1230: \nL1231: Methods:\nL1232: \nL1233: cite87†`set_ticks_per_capture` (ticks_per_capture)  | Sets this RGBCamera to capture a new frame every ticks_per_capture.\nL1234: \nL1235: property data_shapecite85† L1236: \nL1237: \nL1238: The shape of the sensor data\nL1239: \nL1240: Returns:\nL1241: Sensor data shape\nL1242: \nL1243: Return type:\nL1244: \nL1245: \nL1246: `tuple`\nL1247: \nL1248: property dtypecite86† L1249: \nL1250: \nL1251: The type of data in the sensor\nL1252: \nL1253: Returns:\nL1254: \nL1255: \nL1256: Type of sensor data\nL1257: \nL1258: Return type:\nL1259: \nL1260: \nL1261: numpy dtype\nL1262: \nL1263: set_ticks_per_capture(ticks_per_capture)cite87† L1264: \nL1265: \nL1266: Sets this RGBCamera to capture a new frame every ticks_per_capture.\nL1267: \nL1268: The sensor’s image will remain unchanged between captures.\nL1269: \nL1270: This method must be called after every call to env.reset.\nL1271: \nL1272: Parameters:\nL1273: ticks_per_capture (`int`) – The amount of ticks to wait between camera captures.\nL1274: \nL1275: class holoocean.sensors.RangeFinderSensor(client, agent_name, agent_type, name='RangeFinderSensor', config=None)cite21† L1276: \nL1277: \nL1278: Returns distances to nearest collisions in the directions specified by the parameters. If no hit is detected by the laser, a negative value is returned.\nL1279: \nL1280: Configuration\nL1281: \nL1282: The `configuration` block (see cite34†Configuration Block ) accepts the following options:\nL1283:   * `LaserMaxDistance`: Max Distance in meters of RangeFinder. (default 10)\nL1284: \nL1285:   * `LaserCount`: Number of lasers in sensor. Lasers are distributed evenly along the cone defined by laser angle. (default 1)\nL1286: \nL1287:   * `LaserAngle`: Angle of elevation of lasers from origin. Measured in degrees. Straight up would be 90 degrees, down would be -90. (default 0)\nL1288: \nL1289:   * `LaserDebug`: Show debug traces. (default false)\nL1290: \nL1291: Attributes:\nL1292: cite88†`data_shape` | The shape of the sensor data\nL1293: cite89†`dtype` | The type of data in the sensor\nL1294: \nL1295: property data_shapecite88† L1296: \nL1297: \nL1298: The shape of the sensor data\nL1299: \nL1300: Returns:\nL1301: \nL1302: \nL1303: Sensor data shape\nL1304: \nL1305: Return type:\nL1306: \nL1307: \nL1308: `tuple`\nL1309: \nL1310: property dtypecite89† L1311: \nL1312: \nL1313: The type of data in the sensor\nL1314: \nL1315: Returns:\nL1316: \nL1317: \nL1318: Type of sensor data\nL1319: \nL1320: Return type:\nL1321: \nL1322: \nL1323: numpy dtype\nL1324: \nL1325: class holoocean.sensors.RaycastLidar(client, agent_name, agent_type, name='RaycastLidar', config=None)cite22† L1326: \nL1327: \nL1328: Runs Agent’s Lidar\nL1329: Returns a 2D numpy array of shape `[num_points, 5]` where the rows contain:\nL1330: \nL1331:   * `x, y, z`: 3D coordinates of the point in the world in meters (see cite57†Coordinate System )\nL1332: \nL1333:   * `intensity`: Intensity of the point (a float between 0 and 1)\nL1334: \nL1335:   * `ring`: Ring index or channel of the point (0 to number of channels-1)\nL1336: \nL1337: Configuration\nL1338: \nL1339: The `configuration` block (see cite34†Configuration Block ) accepts the following options:\nL1340: \nL1341:   * `Channels` (int): Number of lasers (default: 32)\nL1342:   * `Range` (float): How far each of the lasers can go in meters (default: 500.0)\nL1343: \nL1344:   * `PointsPerSecond` (int): Number of points per second (default: 56000)\nL1345: \nL1346:   * `RotationFrequency` (float): Lidar rotation frequency (default: 10.0)\nL1347: \nL1348:   * `UpperFovLimit` (float): Upper laser angle in degrees, counts from horizontal, positive values mean above horizontal line (default: 10.0)\nL1349:   * `LowerFovLimit` (float): Lower laser angle in degrees, counts from horizontal, negative values mean below horizontal line (default: -30.0)\nL1350: \nL1351:   * `HorizontalFov` (float): Horizontal field of view in degrees (default: 360.0)\nL1352: \nL1353:   * `AtmospAttenRate` (float): Atmospheric attenuation rate in m^-1. Used to calculate atmospheric effects on intensity based on the equation I = e^(-AtmospAttenRate * d) (default: 0.4)\nL1354: \nL1355:   * `RandomSeed` (int): Random seed for noise/drop-off generation (default: 0)\nL1894: Type of sensor data\nL1895: \nL1896: Return type:\nL1897: \nL1898: \nL1899: numpy dtype\nL1900: \nL1901: class holoocean.sensors.ViewportCapture(client, agent_name, agent_type, name='ViewportCapture', config=None)cite31† L1902: \nL1903: \nL1904: Captures what the viewport is seeing.\nL1905: \nL1906: The ViewportCapture is faster than the RGB camera, but there can only be one camera and it must capture what the viewport is capturing. If performance is critical, consider this camera instead of the RGBCamera.\nL1907: \nL1908: It may be useful to position the camera with cite111†`move_viewport()` .\nL1909: \nL1910: Configuration\n------------------------------------------------------------------